germany has reported its highest seven  day incidence rate of covert 19 cases  yet with over with infections registered  at over 900 per 100 000 people for the  first time health authorities recorded  164 000 new cases in a single 24-hour  period  the rise is driven largely by the  dominant omicron variant  the german parliament is set to debate  compulsory vaccines later on later today  the government has already passed  legislation that will make vaccines  required for health care workers from  march  unvaccinated staff could be banned from  work or even fired  we spoke to a nurse who works in a  coveted ward herself but says she won't  get vaccinated whatever the risk  today zabina schroeder is working as an  alternative practitioner she offers her  patients homeopathy treatments  on other days she works as a nurse in an  intensive care unit  when the pandemic started she  volunteered in a coveted ward even local  newspapers reported on this story  it is a smaller intensive care unit but  over 50 of it was occupied by  coronavirus patients  during my shifts i've really experienced  how desperately patients were struggling  for air  that really got to me  from her own experience she knows that  the disease has cost many lives however  she doesn't want to get vaccinated  against covet 19 even though it will be  compulsory for healthcare workers in  germany from march  instead of being vaccinated with a  vaccine whose effect i can't assess i'd  rather catch the virus  of course that could go badly but then i  would rather accept death on my own  terms  lawmaker and dr paola pishota voted for  compulsory vaccinations  she wants to prevent patients from being  infected by nursing staff  for all of us working in healthcare it  is our job that we do not put our  patients at unreasonable risk  and the covet-19 vaccination is  extremely safe which is why it is  acceptable to enforce it to protect our  patients  sabina believes in alternative therapy  not in vaccinations despite all the  scientific proof that they work  most healthcare workers are vaccinated  but zabina could now lose her clinical  job  and my employer would say  we are a danger to the patients  then i will accept that  then i will not work there anymore  she would then be missing out on several  hundred euros a month but that won't  change her mind she says she will remain  unvaccinated  for more let's bring in our political  correspondent emmanuel chaz emma german  parliamentarians today are debating a  blanket mandate for covid19 vaccinations  what are they considering exactly  well terry what's really important to  realize what's happening today is not a  vote it is a round of discussions only  there's no mandatory vaccine that will  be decided here in germany uh today  parliamentarians of all  parties in favor or against the mandate  will debate between themselves on  whether or not they want this mandate to  be voted and on the feasibility  of this mandate they're supported in  that by  president federal president frankvarter  frankwatersteinmeier he had highlighted  a few days ago how important that debate  was he said that of course  the state is under pressure to act  timely in these times of pandemic but  also he said that this didn't replace  the requirement to weigh up  arguments and balance  arguments and balance interests that are  at stake this is precisely what we're  going to see today in the parliament and  it's a very  hardly debated topic also here within  the german population even if two thirds  of the population are actually in favor  of a mandatory vaccine it's not really  uh black and white and maybe let's  listen directly to what some people had  to say about a mandatory vaccine  really it's probably right to make it  mandatory for people who work a lot with  others  from teachers to health care workers  overall i'd say that i'm in favor but  also that i'm not sure  i'm personally against making  vaccination mandatory for some groups of  the population only for example for  health care workers  to me this would be the worst solution  and i say either you make it mandatory  for everyone or for no one  everybody should do it  of course  now there's a big rush but people have  had so much time to get vaccinated  i've also done it and i just think it's  the right thing to do so  i'm in favor of making vaccinations  mandatory because i think that everybody  should watch out for each other and make  sure that we get out of this pandemic at  last  emma if the german parliament does end  up approving a blanket vaccine mandate  do they have any idea how it could be  enforced  well here's uh where it might be  difficult for germany for one  even within the governing coalition it  might be problematic to agree on a  mandatory vaccine while they agree that  a general mandate  could be implemented uh they agree on it  only for  people over 18. for example not all  members of the coalition would also go  for a mandatory vaccine three liberals  virgin kubichi for example  he's the vice president of the bundestag  he warned that a mandatory vaccine would  have little effect on the unvaccinated  here in germany and that it would only  lead in his opinion to a wave of  administrate administrative herders uh  with the fines being issued against  unvaccinated and uh both uh the greens  and the fdp the three uh uh democrats  would be in favor of a mandatory vaccine  from a defined age only to protect the  most vulnerable so they would have to  agree uh on that and of course on  how to implement it within the  population  mandatory vaccines highly controversial  our political correspondent emmanuel  shaz thank you so much  you
 

